---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Blog', href: '/blog/' },
  { label: 'Spring Garden Clearance Guide' },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does garden clearance cost in Bristol?', a: 'Garden clearance in Bristol typically costs £120-£400 depending on the size of the garden and the volume of waste. A small patio clear-up might cost £80-£120, while a large overgrown garden with shed contents could be £300-£500. Green waste, soil, rubble, and non-garden items are all included in our quotes.' },
  { q: 'Can I put garden waste in my household bin in Bristol?', a: 'Bristol City Council provides a green garden waste bin for an annual subscription fee (currently around £46/year). This covers fortnightly collections of grass cuttings, leaves, small branches and weeds. Larger items like branches over 1 metre, tree stumps, soil and rubble cannot go in the green bin and need to go to a recycling centre or be collected by a clearance service.' },
  { q: 'What do you do with garden waste after clearance?', a: 'Green waste is taken to composting facilities where it is turned into soil conditioner and mulch. Woody material goes to biomass processors. Non-organic waste like broken furniture, old pots and garden tools is separated for recycling. We aim to recycle or compost 95% of all garden waste we collect.' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title="Spring Garden Clearance Guide — Get Your Bristol Garden Ready | Bristol House Clearances"
  description="Spring garden clearance tips for Bristol gardens. Clear winter debris, tackle overgrowth and get your outdoor space ready for the warmer months."
  h1="Spring Garden Clearance — Get Your Bristol Garden Ready"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  publishDate="2026-02-25"
  readingTime="3 min"
  relatedGuides={[
    { title: 'Bristol Recycling Centres', href: '/guides/bristol-recycling-centres/' },
    { title: 'Paint & Chemicals Disposal', href: '/guides/paint-chemicals-disposal-bristol/' },
  ]}
  relatedServices={[
    { title: 'Garden Clearance', href: '/garden-clearance/' },
    { title: 'Shed Clearance', href: '/garden-clearance/shed-clearance/' },
    { title: 'Garden Waste Removal', href: '/garden-clearance/garden-waste-removal/' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    After a long Bristol winter, most gardens need some attention. Fallen branches, dead plants, waterlogged debris and months of neglect can leave your outdoor space looking tired and unusable. Spring is the ideal time to clear it all out and start fresh. Here's how to tackle a spring garden clearance — whether you do it yourself or bring in help.
  </p>

  <h2>Assess the Damage</h2>
  <p>
    Before you start, walk around the garden and take stock. Common issues after a Bristol winter include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Fallen branches and storm debris</li>
    <li>Dead or frost-damaged plants that need removing</li>
    <li>Waterlogged areas and compacted soil</li>
    <li>Overgrown hedges, brambles and ivy</li>
    <li>Broken fencing or trellises</li>
    <li>Moss and algae on patios and paths</li>
    <li>A shed full of items that accumulated over winter</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Clear the Green Waste First</h2>
  <p>
    Start by removing dead plants, fallen leaves, broken branches and any organic debris. This clears the way for you to see what you're working with and makes the garden feel immediately better. If you have a Bristol Council green waste bin (around £46/year), use it for smaller amounts. For larger clearances, you'll need a trip to a <a href="/guides/bristol-recycling-centres/">recycling centre</a> or a professional <a href="/garden-clearance/garden-waste-removal/">garden waste removal</a> service.
  </p>

  <h2>Tackle the Shed</h2>
  <p>
    Sheds are where garden clutter goes to hide. Pull everything out and sort into three categories: keep, donate and dispose. Common shed items that need clearing include broken tools, empty pots, old chemicals (these need <a href="/guides/paint-chemicals-disposal-bristol/">specialist disposal</a>), rotting timber and bags of hardened compost. A <a href="/garden-clearance/shed-clearance/">shed clearance</a> can make a dramatic difference to how usable your garden feels.
  </p>

  <h2>Deal with Hard Landscaping</h2>
  <p>
    Broken paving slabs, crumbling walls, old raised beds and unwanted garden structures all create clutter. If you're refreshing the garden, these may need removing. Rubble and soil can go to Bristol recycling centres (small DIY amounts only) or we can collect them as part of a garden clearance.
  </p>

  <h2>Garden Furniture</h2>
  <p>
    Plastic garden furniture that's cracked and faded, rusty metal chairs, rotten wooden benches — spring is the time to replace them. Broken garden furniture can go to the recycling centre or be collected by a clearance service. If items are still usable, consider listing them free on Facebook Marketplace before the summer rush.
  </p>

  <h2>When to Call in Help</h2>
  <p>
    If your garden is severely overgrown, you have a large volume of waste, or you lack the time and physical ability to do it yourself, a professional <a href="/garden-clearance/">garden clearance service</a> handles everything. We clear green waste, non-organic rubbish, shed contents, broken structures and garden furniture in a single visit. All waste is composted, recycled or responsibly disposed of.
  </p>

  <h2>Get Your Garden Ready</h2>
  <p>
    Spring slots fill up fast — everyone wants their garden sorted before the warmer weather arrives. Call us on <strong>0117 123 4567</strong> or <a href="/contact/">get a free quote</a>. Send WhatsApp photos of the garden and we'll price it within hours.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
